  "summary": "Massive Buddhist cave complex featuring monumental stone sculptures carved during the Northern Wei Dynasty, representing the golden age of Chinese Buddhist stone carving.",
  "description": "Characterized by large-scale, imposing figures with robust forms, serene expressions, and detailed drapery that blend Gandharan and Chinese artistic elements. These sculptures were commissioned by imperial patrons and reflect the political and religious significance of Buddhism during this period.",
  "culturalContext": "After the decline of the Western Jin dynasty, the northern parts of China came under the control of the Tuoba-ruled Northern Wei. They made the city of Pingcheng (平城), now known as Datong (大同), their capital. Due to its promotion, Pingcheng saw an increase in construction work. The Northern Wei early adopted Buddhism as their state religion. Buddhism arrived in this location via travel on the ancient North Silk Road, the northernmost route of about 2600 kilometres in length, which connected the ancient Chinese capital of Chang'an to the west over the Wushao Ling Pass to Wuwei and emerging in Kashgar before linking to ancient Parthia.",
